            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did ḍabbu mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ధర",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ధర (dhara) — \"a price\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ధర (dhara) is a price — what a thing costs, said out loud."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is Sanskrit, from a sense of what holds a thing up, which is a fair picture of value. Telugu uses it for one thing's price and for prices in general: when people say the ధర (dhara) has gone up, they mean everything has."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Asking is direct and expected. Nobody in an అంగడి (aṅgaḍi) minds the question, and asking before you pick a thing up is politer than asking after."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three. The question you are allowed to ask first."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
